From 40f1d6434d542680f6aad31127773ba35ecfa6b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:40:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] sidebar, agent_ui: a thread pins to its project as well as
 globally
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next proposal §6.5 (ask 12): a pin is global, as 0049 made it, or
belongs to the thread's project. The active project's pins have a
Pinned section of their own, above the global one.

The pin itself -- whether a thread is pinned, and the order of pins --
stays where 0048 keeps it. Only the scope is new, and it lives in a
database domain of its own (ThreadPinScopeDb, one table,
thread_pin_scope) for the reason 0096 gives: 0048's migration list is
appended to by 0063 under a USE flag, so a step appended there by an
earlier patch would sit at a different index with and without the
flag. A row means "pinned to its project"; no row means global. The
project is the thread's own, read from its metadata when the sidebar
groups it, so it is not stored twice. Like the organization, the scope
belongs to a saved thread (admit) and goes with a deleted one (forget).

Fixed in Zeo 0.10.1, from the operator's first session on 0.10.0. As
shipped, pinning was global by default, "Pin to Project" was the extra
entry, and a project pin only moved to the top of its group, with no
section. The operator asked for the opposite default and for a
section:

- Every pin entry point -- the row's button, the keyboard action and
  "Pin Thread" -- pins to the project (toggle_project_pin). The other
  scope is one menu entry away, for an unpinned thread and a pinned one
  alike: "Pin Globally", or "Pin to Project" on a global pin. Changing
  scope keeps the thread's place in the pin order.
- There are two Pinned sections after the last group. "Pinned" lists
  the active project's pins (project_pins::active_project_pin_ids: the
  project pins whose thread derives the active group's key); "Pinned
  Globally" under it lists the global ones, as 0049's section did. One
  project section, not one per group: the operator chose it. A project
  pin of any other project stays an ordinary row of its group until
  that project is the active one.
- ListEntry::PinnedHeader carries which section it heads, and each
  section collapses on its own, under its own key-value key. The
  hoist inside the group is gone.

The scope is written only after the organization accepted the toggle:
a refused toggle changes nothing and still warns once. An unpin clears
the scope, so a later global pin does not inherit it.

Tests: pin_scope::tests (a migration list of its own; a project pin
survives a restart and global clears it; a draft has none; a deleted
thread's is forgotten), project_pins::tests (only the active project's
pins are listed, in pin order; no active project and unknown threads
list nothing) and sidebar_pinned_tests::project_pins (the default pin
is the project's and its section sits above the global one; a project
pin shows its section only while its project is active; each section
collapses on its own; unpinning clears the scope; the menu offers the
other scope). Seen failing under two probes: the default toggle
pinning globally, and the section ignoring which project is active.
sidebar_tags_tests anchors on "Pin Globally", now the entry before
Tags.
---

